{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["And they were a bitterness of spirit — because Scripture will say: 'If Jacob takes a wife of the daughters of Heth like these,' it was therefore necessary to explain first: מרת רוח — like 'one who does'; for the masculine one says עושה דברו, for the feminine [one says עושה]. And when it is in construct state one says עושת. So too from the form מורה one gets the form ממרה, as in 'if one rebels against your mouth' — for the feminine one says מורה, and when it is in construct state one says מורת רוח, a contrariant. But 'but the heart knows the bitterness of its soul' — it takes a qamatz because it is a noun, in the sense of bitternesses. But this one, with a holem, is a verb form from the paradigm 'כי מרה מריתי,' from the pa'al formations with a dropped nun."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The lemma מרת רוח appears twice: as a verb (participle) in our verse with a holem-vav, and as a noun in the Proverbs quotation with a qamatz. קונט\"רריאנ\"ץ appears to be a transliterated Old French gloss; the word is left untranslated as in the manuscript. The grammatical discussion hinges on Rashbam's analysis of מר as deriving from a root with quiesced nun (למ\"ד used here in the medieval sense of the third radical being a dropped letter). The bracketed עושה is inferred from the feminine context; the Hebrew says only לנקיבה with no explicit form given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:45:26.871933193Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}